# Blacksmith CI_RUNNER auto-flip (fleet-ops#8936). One hourly decision on a
# cheap seat: does this calendar month still have Blacksmith free minutes left,
# and does the org Actions variable CI_RUNNER point at Blacksmith accordingly?
#
# Stock only, no glue: one prompt file (prompts/blacksmith-flip.yml, installed to
# the stable path fleet-sync's step 9 writes), piped into `pi --print` on the
# cheap worker-cheap seat. The model runs `blacksmith usage`, compares the
# largest of its three minute columns against 2,850 (95% of the 3,000 free
# minutes, 4-vCPU minutes counting double), then runs exactly one of
# `gh variable set CI_RUNNER --org Nishfleet --body blacksmith-4vcpu-ubuntu-2404`
# or `gh variable delete CI_RUNNER --org Nishfleet`.
#
# Why a model rather than a shell script: "no glue or scripts" was Nish's
# condition on approval (issue #8936), and the prompt is the whole decision.
#
# gh auth: this unit runs under the systemd --user manager, which has no
# GH_TOKEN, so `gh` reads ~/.config/gh/hosts.yml and acts as the nish3451
# account (admin:org) — the one identity allowed to write org variables. No
# credential is in this file, in the prompt, or in argv.
#
# FLEET_TASK_ID: models.json's litellm provider sends it as the
# x-litellm-session-id header, so without it every call fails with
# "Failed to resolve provider litellm header" (proved 2026-10-01, first run).
# A fixed id is correct here: this is a recurring check, not a per-task agent.
#
# --no-context-files and the trailing grep are the two halves of one fact
# (proved on four live runs, 2026-10-01):
#   * With AGENTS.md loaded, a run stopped itself and printed
#     "BLOCKED: money/pricing reserved class (needs Nish approval) + GH_TOKEN
#     empty (hard stop)" and FLIP: ERROR. AGENTS.md is the worker's own
#     operating contract: a minutes guard with no human in the loop must not
#     abstain on a rule about reaching a human, and `gh` here is
#     authenticated from hosts.yml, not from GH_TOKEN. Loading the worker
#     contract into a 40-second scheduled check imports those stop conditions
#     and nothing else it needs.
#   * The prompt's last line is FLIP: SET or FLIP: DELETE, and nothing else is
#     accepted. `pi --print` exits 0 whatever the model decides, so without
#     this check a failed CLI call, an unparseable JSON body or a refusal all
#     recorded a SUCCESSFUL hourly flip while leaving the variable stale.
#     Exiting 1 is what puts the unit in `systemctl --user list-units
#     --state=failed` (issue #8936 bullet 5).
[Unit]
Description=Blacksmith CI_RUNNER auto-flip (hourly)
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
Environment=FLEET_TASK_ID=blacksmith-flip-hourly
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'out=$(cat /home/nish/.local/share/blacksmith-flip/prompt.yml | /home/nish/.local/bin/pi --print --provider litellm --model worker-cheap --no-context-files); printf "%s\n" "$out"; printf "%s\n" "$out" | grep -qE "^FLIP: (SET|DELETE)$" || exit 1'
TimeoutStartSec=300